ruberant growth pattern. Histological appearance showing serrated epithelial architecture in association with features of conventional dysplasia (nuclear crowding and pencillate nuclei).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="ctg20115f2"/></fig><table-wrap id="tbl1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><title>Prevalence, location, and size of polyps according to histology</title></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups" border="1"><colgroup><col align="left"/><col align="char" char="("/><col align="char" char="."/><col align="char" char="("/><col align="char" char="("/><col align="char" char="("/><col align="char" char="("/><col align="char" char="("/></colgroup><thead valign="bottom"><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50"><bold>Histology</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>Subjects (&#x00025; <italic>n</italic>=220)</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="." charoff="50"><bold>Number (<italic>n</italic>=258)</bold></th><th colspan="2" align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>Location (&#x00025;)</bold><hr/></th><th colspan="3" align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>Size (&#x00025;)</bold><hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="char" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="char" valign="top" char="." charoff="50">&#x000a0;</th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>Proximal</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>Distal</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>&#x02264;5&#x02009;mm</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>6&#x02013;9&#x02009;mm</bold></th><th align="center" valign="top" char="(" charoff="50"><bold>&#x02265;10&#x02009;mm</bold></th></tr></thead><tbody valign="top"><tr><td align="left" valign="top" charoff="50">Hyperplastic</td><td align="char" valign="top"